   after P1.2, P1.3 finds 1 site(s) reserved wider than it needs

-- T1: the encoder's output does not depend on its own history --
   Sequential, one thread. The learned width survives a reset by design, so
   this asks whether what survives can be seen on the wire. Needs two shapes.

   12 pairs checked on two arms.

-- T2 and T3: the same work, run in sequence and then together --
   4 threads x 6 rounds x 4 shapes, every encode memcmp'd against the
   reference. T2 joins each thread before starting the next, so the two
   differ only by overlap: a defect that shows in both is not a concurrency
   defect, and that distinction is why 12.5 asks for both.

  T2 threads in sequence                   96 encodes   native 0  ffi 0  ffi-hosttc 0  roundtrip 0  spurious-err 0
  T3 threads together                      96 encodes   native 0  ffi 0  ffi-hosttc 0  roundtrip 0  spurious-err 0

-- T4: a failure on one thread is not visible on another --
   Half the threads feed truncated input and must fail; half feed the
   reference and must succeed with err == 0. Concurrently, 24 rounds.

   truncated-but-accepted 0, good-but-wrong 0, error leaked across a context 0

-- T5: two threads with different histories encode the same message --
   Every ordered pair: thread A encodes the prior and then the target,
   thread B encodes the target alone. Both must produce the reference AND
   agree with each other -- agreement is the half that a manifest comparison
   alone would miss if both drifted the same way, which is exactly what a
   process-global table does.

   12 ordered pairs x 12 rounds: disagreements 0, wrong against the reference 0

-- T6: one shape against two, same threads, same rounds --
   12.5: "a suite with one shape reports zero wrong bytes with a
   per-thread-state defect present and absent alike; two shapes find it in
   twenty encodes out of twenty." That is a claim, so it is measured. On the
   shipped design every row below is zero; the three planted builds are where
   the row that matters is not.

