concurrency: 0 build(s) did not do what they must

================================================================================
WHAT THIS MEASURED
================================================================================

1. The shipped design passes every axis, at C++17 and at the C++11 floor, on both
   linkages, and with four times more threads than the machine has cores.

2. 12.5's last sentence, which is a claim and is therefore measured rather than
   quoted. "A suite with one shape reports zero wrong bytes with a per-thread-state
   defect present and absent alike; two shapes find it in twenty encodes out of
   twenty." On the AK_CONC_PAD build:

     one shape  (P1.1 alone)        24 encodes,    0 wrong
     one shape  (P1.2 alone)        24 encodes,    0 wrong
     two shapes (P1.1 + P1.2)       48 encodes,   44 wrong
     four shapes (P1.x + P2.x)      96 encodes,   46 wrong

   The sentence holds, and the mechanism behind it is sharper than the sentence: it
   is not "two shapes" that matters but two shapes that WANT DIFFERENT WIDTHS AT A
   SHARED LENGTH-PREFIX SITE. P1.1 and P1.2 are the same message type at 858 B and
   218 KB and they learn the SAME table -- the prefixes that vary are per element and
   the elements are the same size in both, only the count differs, and a top-level
   message carries no prefix at all. Two shapes of different message types touch
   disjoint sites and can never over-reserve for each other. The pair that works is
   P1.1 and P1.3: one message type, one of them the absent-path payload, so a body
   that needs two bytes in P1.1 needs one in P1.3.

   This slice's first version of the suite used P1.1, P1.2, P2.1, P2.2 and PASSED ON
   ALL THREE PLANTED BUILDS. T0 exists because of that: it asks the encoder which
   ordered pairs have a history surface at all, and prints the answer whether or not
   it is empty, because an empty one means T1, T5 and T6 are testing nothing.

3. Section 6's two refusals are INDEPENDENT, and only their combination corrupts:

     build                     bytes wrong   two threads disagree   T7 scaling (b)
     shipped                             0                      0      3.63 - 3.96x
     AK_CONC_PAD                        46                     16               n/a
     AK_CONC_GLOBAL                      0                      0      2.80 - 2.87x
     AK_CONC_PAD + GLOBAL               46                      0               n/a

   A process-global width table on its own is a data race and a throughput defect and
   NOT a byte defect: an unpadded prefix is rewritten to the width the body actually
   needs, whatever the guess was, so a torn read costs a move and not a wrong byte.
   That is why conc_a17_global is in gen/concurrency.sh's must-PASS list.

   Padding to the learned width on its own IS a byte defect, and it is the one that
   makes two threads disagree (16 of 16): thread A has a history and thread B does
   not, so they emit two different legal encodings of the same message -- section 6's
   sentence, reproduced.

   Both together is the worst case AND the one a naive suite would miss: the threads
   AGREE (0 disagreements) because they share the polluted table, and they are both
   wrong. A suite that only asked "do two threads agree?" would pass here. Only the
   comparison against an independent reference catches it, which is why the oracle is
   protobuf's encoder and not a re-encode with the codec under test.

4. Section 6's throughput claim -- "a global table made two encoding threads slower
   than one" -- reproduced from C++, and refined. Scaling is a ratio taken inside one
   process, so comparing two builds by their scaling does not carry R4's 0.240
   across-build bar. Three runs per build, because the single runs inside the sweep
   above disagreed with each other on leg (a) by more than the effect; the three-run
   block is at the end of this log and these are its ranges.

     leg (a): every thread on ONE shape, so the table is written only on a miss and is
              read-mostly and shared clean after the first few iterations
     leg (b): threads alternate between shapes that want DIFFERENT WIDTHS at the same
              site, so the table is written on every encode

                              shipped          AK_CONC_GLOBAL      global is
     (a) scaling        3.89 - 4.00x           3.83 - 3.85x
     (a) aggregate    15.5 - 15.9 M/s       12.9 - 13.7 M/s     1.13 - 1.23x slower
     (b) scaling        3.63 - 3.96x           2.80 - 2.87x
     (b) aggregate    2.91 - 3.15 M/s       1.54 - 1.59 M/s     1.83 - 2.05x slower

   1.83 to 2.05 on the contended leg, inside the java slice's measured 1.32 to 2.23,
   from a different language and a different machine. And the refinement: leg (a) shows
   1.13 to 1.23 and its SCALING does not degrade at all. Measuring only leg (a) would
   have reported "a global table costs about 15 percent and scales fine". The cost is a
   function of how often the table is WRITTEN, which is how often two threads want
   different widths at one site -- so section 6's sentence is right and its mechanism is
   narrower than "sharing is slow".

WHAT IS NOT MEASURED HERE

  * A thread sanitizer run. The core is a Rust cdylib built without TSan, so a TSan
    build of the host would report the core's internals as uninstrumented and the
    result would be noise. The AK_CONC_GLOBAL race is therefore argued from the code
    and its throughput, not from a race detector.
  * A shared CONTEXT. ABI v1 makes ak_enc_ctx host-owned and every thread here owns
    its own; passing one context to two threads is not a supported use and is not
    tested as though it were.
  * The RPC half. The rust slice's shared-mutable-client defect (section 9) is what
    motivated 12.5, and this suite covers the CODEC rather than the client handle.
  * Anything about the core's own internals under contention: the ffi arm is exercised
    and passes on every axis, but the plants are in this slice's ak::Enc, because the
    core is shared (R0) and planting a defect in it is not a slice's to do.

